<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">narration</ansi>

How to read a fight. Combat is told as a story, one line per blow, and the
numbers that matter sit in brackets at the end of the line.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What the brackets mean</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">(5 damage)</ansi>                a hit, and the health it took
  <ansi fg="command">(critical hit, 9 damage)</ansi>  a critical hit: it lands harder
  <ansi fg="command">(critical hit, 9 damage, bleeding)</ansi>  and it left a status (<ansi fg="command">help statuses</ansi>)
  <ansi fg="command">(1 damage, bleeding)</ansi>  a status doing its damage, on the victim's turn
  <ansi fg="command">(5 damage, 2 blocked)</ansi>     a hit on you; your armor stopped 2 of it
  <ansi fg="command">(4 healed)</ansi>                health a spell gave back
  <ansi fg="command">(chanting: Magic Missile, 2 rounds)</ansi>
                            a spell being cast, and the rounds left
                            before it is released

A line with nothing in brackets is a miss, a dodge, or someone getting
ready. Every miss is shown, yours and theirs.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">A critical hit</ansi>

Only a real critical hit is called one, and it reads like one. A strong
blow that isn't critical reads as a clean, heavy hit. Your chance of a
critical hit comes from your smarts (<ansi fg="command">help smarts</ansi>), and how hard it lands
from your perception (<ansi fg="command">help perception</ansi>).

When a critical hit does damage and the victim stays standing, a pain reaction
follows the hit line. You read your own pain as "you"; everyone else sees the
victim react by name. A normal hit, a fully blocked critical hit, or a dodge
has no pain reaction. When a critical hit drops the victim, the death line
follows instead. Pain reactions are words only: they add no damage or status.
A short pause comes before a pain reaction or a death line, so the blow lands
first (<ansi fg="command">help combatpace</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">How a fight reads</ansi>

- One line opens the fight, when a group comes at you or you at them.
- When anyone takes a new target, you see it: "The bandit captain turns
  toward Garrick Vane."
- When a foe falls, you see it fall, in the order it happened.
- When the last foe falls, one line closes the fight, and then your battle
  summary follows (<ansi fg="command">help battle-summary</ansi>).
- A spell shows its chant each round, then one line when it is released.
  A spell that heals several people lists them all on one line:
  "Garrick Vane (3 healed) · Ysolde (2 healed) · you (4 healed)".

Names of people are written as they are ("Garrick Vane"); everything else
gets "the" ("the bandit captain").

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Pronouns and repeated enemy names</ansi>

People and creatures use their own pronouns: he/him/his, she/her/her,
they/them/their, or it/it/its. Tamsin Reed prays with a glow in her hands;
Brother Oswin uses his. Generated recruits use they. Your own lines still
say you and your; other people see they when a line refers to you.

Two foes with the same name are given fixed labels, such as the first
cutthroat and the second cutthroat. If the first falls, the survivor stays
second. A newcomer is the third cutthroat: fallen foes keep their numbers
reserved until the battle ends. A foe that began alone keeps its original
name even if another of its kind joins later. Different kinds with the
same short name use fuller labels to keep them distinct.

The labels agree in blows, spells, target changes and your battle summary.
Players fighting the same foes in the same room see the same labels.
These labels help you follow the story; start a fight with attack [group]
as before. Room descriptions, scouting and group selection use their
ordinary names. Player pronoun selection is reserved for a later phase.

A new battle starts its labels afresh. After a server restart or copyover,
newly rebuilt battles also start afresh; the old ordering and summary are
not retained.

See also <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help damage</ansi>, and <ansi fg="command">help cast</ansi>.
